xample, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is tic-tac-toe, and I'm playing as O. The other player just placed their mark at (1,1), which is the center. Now it's my turn. The current board state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the second row has an X in the middle. The empty cells are all the others. My goal is to block the opponent's potential wins and create my own opportunities.\n\nFirst, I need to check if there's any immediate threat. The opponent has an X in the center. If they continue to play, they might try to form a line. But since the board is mostly empty, maybe I should focus on controlling the center or corners.\n\nWait, the opponent's move was the center. That's a strategic move. Now, I need to respond. The legal actions are all cells except (1,1), which is already taken. So I can choose any of the other cells.\n\nI should consider possible moves that can block the opponent. For example, if the opponent is trying to make a row, column, or diagonal. But with only one X, they can't have a line yet. But maybe they are setting up for a future move.\n\nAlternatively, I should try