[2026-09-24 01:05:23] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 5310] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's start by thinking about the best first move in tic-tac-toe. Since the board is completely empty, I need to choose a position that gives me the best chance to win. The center is usually a good spot because it's part of multiple potential lines (rows, columns, diagonals). So maybe I should go for the center, which is (1,1). That way, I can try to create multiple opportunities for winning. Alternatively, corner positions are also good because they can lead to diagonals. But the center is more versatile. Let me pick the center. So the action would be X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's start by thinking about the best first move in tic-tac-toe. Since the board is completely empty, I need to choose a position that gives me the best chance to win. The center is usually a good spot because it's part of multiple potential lines (rows, columns, diagonals). So maybe I should go for the center, which is (1,1). That way, I can try to create multiple opportunities for winning. Alternatively, corner positions are also good because they can lead to diagonals. But the center is more versatile. Let me pick the center. So the action would be X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer><|im_end|><|im_start|>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game state after Turn 1 is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has an X in the center (1,1). It's my turn to place an O. The legal actions are all the empty cells except (1,1) which is already taken. I need to choose a move that's optimal.\n\nFirst, I should check if there's any immediate threat from X. Since X just placed in the center, they might be trying to form a line. But with only one X, they can't have a winning line yet. My goal is to block any potential wins and also set up my own.\n\nLooking at the board, the current state is:\n\nRow 0: all underscores\nRow 1: _ X _\nRow 2: all underscores\n\nSo the center is X. The possible moves for O are all other cells. Let me think about possible strategies. Since X is in the center, they might be aiming for a diagonal or a row/column. But with only one X, they can't win yet. However, I need to block any possible lines they can form.\n\nFor example, if X places another mark in a corner, they could form a diagonal. But since it's my turn, I should block any potential threats. Alternatively, I can try to create my own opportunities.\n\nLet me consider possible moves. If I place O in (0,0), that's a corner. Then, if X later places in (0,1) or (1,0), they could form a line. But maybe that's not the best move. Alternatively, placing O in (0,1) or (2,0) might be better.\n\nWait, maybe I should look for the best move to block X's possible next moves. Since X is in the center, if they place in (0,1) next, they could form a row. But since it's my turn, I need to choose a move that either blocks X or starts my own line.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take a central position. But the center is already taken by X. So I can't. So the options are corners or edges.\n\nAnother approach: the game is in the early stages. The best strategy is to take the center if possible, but since it's already taken by X, I need to take another position. Maybe I should take a corner to create a diagonal. For example, if I take (0,0), then if X takes (0,1), I can take (0,2) to complete the diagonal. But that's a bit far.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should block X's possible moves. For example, if X is going to take (0,1) next, I should block that. But since X's next move is not known, I need to choose a move that is the most strategic.\n\nAnother thought: in the first move, X took the center. Then, as O, I should take a corner. That's a common strategy. For example, taking (0,0) would be a good move. Then, if X tries to take a corner, I can block them. Alternatively, taking (0,0) allows me to create a diagonal with possible future moves.\n\nSo, the best move here is probably to take a corner. Let's say (0,0). That would be <O(0,0)>. Alternatively, maybe (2,2) is also a good move. But since the board is symmetric, either is fine. But the first move is to take a corner. So I'll choose (0,0).\n</think>\n\n<O(0,0)>", "episode_score": -11.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game state after Turn 1 is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has an X in the center (1,1). It's my turn to place an O. The legal actions are all the empty cells except (1,1) which is already taken. I need to choose a move that's optimal.\n\nFirst, I should check if there's any immediate threat from X. Since X just placed in the center, they might be trying to form a line. But with only one X, they can't have a winning line yet. My goal is to block any potential wins and also set up my own.\n\nLooking at the board, the current state is:\n\nRow 0: all underscores\nRow 1: _ X _\nRow 2: all underscores\n\nSo the center is X. The possible moves for O are all