[2026-09-24 01:33:11] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 5310] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is crucial because I want to take control of the center or maybe a corner to maximize possibilities. But since the board is empty, maybe I should go for the center. Wait, but the center is (1,1). Alternatively, corners are good because they can lead to multiple lines. Let me think. The first move is usually to take the center, but sometimes people start with a corner. But since I'm trying to win, maybe center is better. Wait, but the first move doesn't have any immediate threats. Let me check the legal actions. All cells are available. So I can choose any. But what's the optimal first move? In tic-tac-toe, the best strategy is to take the center. So I'll go with (1,1). That way, I can create multiple potential lines. So the answer should be X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is crucial because I want to take control of the center or maybe a corner to maximize possibilities. But since the board is empty, maybe I should go for the center. Wait, but the center is (1,1). Alternatively, corners are good because they can lead to multiple lines. Let me think. The first move is usually to take the center, but sometimes people start with a corner. But since I'm trying to win, maybe center is better. Wait, but the first move doesn't have any immediate threats. Let me check the legal actions. All cells are available. So I can choose any. But what's the optimal first move? In tic-tac-toe, the best strategy is to take the center. So I'll go with (1,1). That way, I can create multiple potential lines. So the answer should be X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer><|im_end|><|im_start|>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is tic-tac-toe, and I'm playing as O. The other player is X. Let me look at the current game state.\n\nIn Turn 1, the other player (X) placed their mark in the center, which is (1,1). So the board now has an X in the middle. Now it's my turn. The current game state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the first row is all empty, the second row has an X in the middle, and the third row is all empty. The legal actions for me are to place an O in any of the remaining cells except (1,1), which is already taken.\n\nI need to choose a move that's optimal. Let me think about possible strategies. Since X went for the center, maybe I should block them or try to create a winning position. But since it's the first move for O, maybe I should try to take control of the corners or edges.\n\nLooking at the board, the available cells are all except (1,1). The possible moves are the 8 surrounding cells. Let me consider possible moves. If I place O in a corner, like (0,0), that might help me set up for future moves. Alternatively, maybe blocking X's possible lines.\n\nBut X is the other player, and they might be trying to create a line. Since they have an X in the center, they could be aiming for a diagonal or a row or column. For example, if X takes (0,1) next, they could form a line. But since it's my turn, I need to block that.\n\nWait, but in the current state, the only X is in the center. So X's possible next moves could be to take corners or edges. But since it's my turn, I need to choose a position that either blocks X or starts a potential line for me.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take a corner. Let's say I take (0,0). That would make the top-left corner O. Then, if X tries to take the top-middle, I can block. Or if X takes the middle-top, I can block. But maybe that's too early.\n\nAlternatively, maybe taking the center is not possible, but since it's already taken by X. So maybe the best move is to take a corner. Let's say (0,0). That's a common strategy to start with. Alternatively, maybe (0,2) or (2,0) or (2,2). Any of those would be good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take the center, but it's already taken. So the next best thing is to take a corner. So I'll choose (0,0) as my move. That way, I can control the top-left corner, and maybe set up for a diagonal. Let me check if that's a good move.\n\nAlternatively, maybe (0,1) is a bad idea because that's adjacent to the X in the center. If I take (0,1), then X could take (0,2) or (1,1) (but that's already taken). Wait, but (0,1) is adjacent to the center. If I take (0,1), then X could try to take (0,2) or (1,0) or (2,1) to make a line. But maybe that's not the best.\n\nAlternatively, taking (0,0) would block X from taking that corner. So I think taking a corner is better. Let me choose (0,0). So the action would be <O(0,0)>. That's the first move. So the answer is that.\n</think>\n\n<O(0,0)>", "episode_score": -11.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is tic-tac-toe, and I'm playing as O. The other player