[2026-09-24 01:50:44] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 5310] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is for me. Let me think about the best strategy here.\n\nTic-tac-toe is a game where the first player (X) has a good chance if they play optimally. The key is to take the center, then occupy corners, and so on. But since it's the first move, I should probably take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good starting point because it's part of multiple lines. Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe starting at a corner is better. But I remember that the optimal first move is the center. Let me confirm. If I take the center (1,1), that gives me more opportunities to create lines. So the first move should be X(1,1). That makes sense. Let me choose that.\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is for me. Let me think about the best strategy here.\n\nTic-tac-toe is a game where the first player (X) has a good chance if they play optimally. The key is to take the center, then occupy corners, and so on. But since it's the first move, I should probably take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good starting point because it's part of multiple lines. Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe starting at a corner is better. But I remember that the optimal first move is the center. Let me confirm. If I take the center (1,1), that gives me more opportunities to create lines. So the first move should be X(1,1). That makes sense. Let me choose that.\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer><|im_end|><|im_start|>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is in Turn 2, and it's my turn as O. The current game state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has the X in the center (1,1). The legal actions for me are all the empty cells except (1,1), which is already taken by X. My goal is to block X from winning and try to create my own winning line.\n\nFirst, I need to check if X is about to win in the next move. Since X just placed in the center, they might be trying to complete a line. Let's think about possible moves X could make. If X is trying to make a diagonal, maybe they could complete a diagonal by placing in (0,0) or (2,2). But since it's my turn, I need to block that.\n\nWait, in the current state, X is in the center. If X places in (0,0) next, then they could have a diagonal with (0,0), (1,1), (2,2). But since it's my turn, I need to prevent that. Alternatively, if X is trying to make a vertical line, but they only have one in the center. Maybe they can't win immediately.\n\nBut since it's my turn, I should place my O in a position that blocks X's possible wins. Let's check possible moves. If I place in (0,0), that would block X from completing the diagonal. Alternatively, placing in (2,2) would do the same. But maybe there's a better move.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should try to create my own two in a row. Let's see. The current board has X in (1,1). If I place in (0,0) and (0,2), but that's not possible in one move. Wait, in this turn, I can only place one O. So maybe I should block X's possible win.\n\nLooking at the possible moves for X next turn: if X is to place in (0,0), then they can complete the diagonal. So I should block that by placing O in (0,0) or (2,2). But since the legal actions for me are all except (1,1), I can choose either (0,0) or (2,2) to block that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe X is trying to make a horizontal or vertical line. But with only one X, that's not possible. So the main threat is the diagonal. So I should block that. Let's choose (0,0) or (2,2). Let's pick (0,0). But wait, maybe the best move is to take the center? No, the center is already taken by X. So the options are the corners or edges.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take a corner to create a potential for my own lines. For example, if I take (0,0), then I can later connect with other moves. But since the current state is just the X in center, maybe blocking is better.\n\nSo the best move is to block the diagonal by placing O in (0,0) or (2,2). Let's pick (0,0). So the action would be <O(0,0)>. Alternatively, maybe (2,2) is better? But both are equivalent. Let me check the possible moves again.\n\nThe legal actions are O(0,0), O(0,1), O(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2). So all except (1,1). So I can choose any of these. But to block X's possible win, I should take either (0,0) or (2,2). Let's pick (0,0). So the answer is <O(0,0)>.\n</think>\n\n<O(0,0)>", "episode_score": -11.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is in Turn 2, and it's my turn as O. The current game state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has the X in the center (1,1). The legal actions for me are all the empty cells except (1,1), which is already taken by X. My goal is to block X from winning and try to create my own winning line.\n\nFirst