ck that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe taking the middle of the side. For example, (0,1) is the middle of the top row. If I take that, then maybe I can create a line with other moves. But I need to think about the best move here.\n\nIn tic-tac-toe, the optimal strategy is to take the center if possible, but since the center is already taken by X, I need to choose another position. The best move for O here would be to take a corner, which is a common strategy to start forming a line or block the opponent.\n\nSo, let me choose (0,0). That's a corner. Then, the board becomes:\n\nO _ _\n_X_\n___\n\nThat would block X from making a diagonal if they take (0,2) or (2,2). Alternatively, maybe taking (2,0) or (2,2) would be similar. But since the first row is empty, taking (0,0) is a good move. Alternatively, maybe taking (1,0) or (1,2) to block X's possible moves.\n\nWait, X is in the center. If X takes a corner next, they can make a diagonal. So I need to block that. So taking a corner now would be a good idea. Let me go with (0,0).\n</think>\n\n<O(0,0)><|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based o