ly one X, they can't form a line yet. So maybe my best move is to take a corner to start building my own lines.\n\nLet me check the possible moves. The available cells are all except (1,1). Let me think about the possible moves. If I take (0,0), then the board becomes O in (0,0), X in (1,1). Then the next move could be to take (0,2) or (2,0) or (2,2) to create a corner. Alternatively, maybe (0,1) or (1,0) to block.\n\nBut since the opponent is X, maybe I should focus on corners. Let's say I take (0,0). Then the next possible moves could be to take (0,2) or (2,0) or (2,2). Alternatively, if I take (0,1), that's the middle of the top row. But that might not be as good as a corner.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take (0,0) to start controlling the corners. So the answer would be O(0,0). Or maybe (2,2)? Either way, but I need to choose one. Alternatively, maybe (1,0) or (1,2) to block the center. Wait, but the center is already taken. So maybe taking a corner is better.\n\nSo I think the best move is to take a corner. Let's pick (0,0). So the action is <O(0,0)>.\n</think>\n\n<O(0,0)><|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an