is to place in an empty cell. So if O places in (1,0), that would block the middle row. Similarly, placing in (1,2) would block the middle row. But since the middle row is row 1, which is _ X _, so if O places in (1,0) or (1,2), that would block the middle row. But that's not possible because the middle row is already X in the center. Wait, no. The middle row is row 1, which has X in the center. If O places in (1,0), that would be the left cell of the middle row. Then, if X plays in (1,2), they can complete the middle row. But O can block that by placing in (1,2) in their next turn. But since it's O's turn now, maybe O should block the middle row by placing in (1,0) or (1,2). Wait, but that's not blocking the row, because the row is already partially filled. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take (0,1) to block the middle column. Because if X plays in (0,1), they can complete the middle column. So O should block that by placing in (0,1) or (2,1). So placing in (0,1) is a good move. Similarly, placing in (2,1) would block the middle column.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take (0,0) to block the diagonal. But that's less critical unless X is trying to make that diagonal.\n\nSo, considering all possibilities, the optimal move for O is to block the middle column by placing in (0,1) or (2,1). Let's choose (0,1). That would be O(0,1). Alternatively, (2,1). But which one is better?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take the center, but it's already taken by X. So no. So the best is to block the middle column. So O(0,1) or O(2,1). Let's pick O(0,1). So the answer would be <O(0,1)>.\n</think>\n\n<O(0,1)><|im_end|>"}]

